{{#if isNodeBackend}}
## {{nodeBackendDisplayName}} Backend

This project keeps Expo at the root and adds a private TypeScript server in `apps/server`.
Copy the committed examples before starting local development:

```bash
cp .env.example .env.local
cp apps/server/.env.example apps/server/.env.local
```

`EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL` is embedded in the mobile bundle and must be an absolute Backend URL reachable by the target device. Replace the example address with your development machine's LAN address for physical devices, use `http://10.0.2.2:3000` for the Android emulator, or use `http://127.0.0.1:3000` for the iOS simulator. Production builds require the deployed HTTPS Backend URL. `PORT`, `CLIENT_ORIGIN`{{#if hasDatabaseWorkspace}}, and `DATABASE_URL`{{/if}} belong only to the {{nodeBackendDisplayName}} server. Keep real local environment files uncommitted.

{{#if isClerkAuth}}
Clerk owns Email and Password identity, required Email verification, sessions, and token signing. Enable Clerk's Native API, required Email with verification code, Password sign-up, and Device Trust in a Clerk application you own. Put its Publishable Key in the root `EXPO_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` and server `CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` values. Put its Secret Key only in the server `CLERK_SECRET_KEY` value. The generated Expo routes use Clerk's SecureStore token cache, restore sessions, forward bearer tokens, and return to Auth routes after sign-out. Never commit real keys or `.env.local` files.
{{/if}}
{{#if isBetterAuth}}
Better Auth owns Email and Password identity and durable cookie sessions in {{#if isMysqlDatabase}}MySQL{{else}}PostgreSQL{{/if}}. Generate a random `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` of at least 32 characters, keep it only in `apps/server/.env.local`, and set `BETTER_AUTH_URL` to the public Express origin. Registration creates an immediate durable session. The generated Expo client restores the SecureStore-backed session, forwards its cookie to protected Business Data requests, and returns to Auth routes after sign-out. Never commit the real secret or `.env.local` files.
{{/if}}

{{#if hasDatabaseWorkspace}}
{{#if isMysqlDatabase}}MySQL{{else}}PostgreSQL{{/if}} and {{#if isPrismaOrm}}Prisma{{else}}Drizzle{{/if}} live in `packages/db`. Create an empty {{#if isMysqlDatabase}}MySQL{{else}}PostgreSQL{{/if}} database, set its connection URL only in `apps/server/.env.local`, then apply the committed migration, seed Setup Type-specific Starter Data, {{#if isPrismaOrm}}generate Prisma Client, {{/if}}and build the Database package:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} db:setup
```

Run this setup before the first Backend start and during deployment before starting compiled output. The Database contains Tenkit App Variant, Runtime Tenant, and Runtime Tenant Access tables{{#if isBetterAuth}}, plus Better Auth User, Session, Account, and Verification tables{{/if}}{{#if isPrismaOrm}} and Prisma migration metadata{{/if}}. See `packages/db/{{#if isMysqlDatabase}}MYSQL{{else}}POSTGRESQL{{/if}}.md` for provider guidance.
{{/if}}

Run Expo and {{nodeBackendDisplayName}} together:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} dev
```

Expo-only `{{packageManagerRunCommand}} start` remains available. Verify the server with `GET http://127.0.0.1:3000/health`, run its tests with `{{packageManagerRunCommand}} test`, and prove compiled production startup with:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} build
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} server:start:prod
```
{{/if}}

{{#if isConvexBackend}}
## Convex Backend

This project keeps Expo at the root and adds Convex-owned schema, functions, Starter Data, generated support, and tests in `apps/server`. Convex manages persistence, so no SQL Database, ORM, or Node production process is part of this project.

Copy the committed examples, then connect only to a Convex development deployment you own:

```bash
cp .env.example .env.local
cp apps/server/.env.example apps/server/.env.local
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} convex:sync
```

The Convex CLI writes deployment metadata to the ignored `apps/server/.env.local`. Set `EXPO_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL` in the root `.env.local` to that deployment's public HTTPS URL, then seed Convex-owned Starter Data and run Expo with Convex sync:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} convex:seed
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} dev
```

Use `{{packageManagerRunCommand}} convex:codegen` to refresh committed generated support, `{{packageManagerRunCommand}} convex:sync` for a one-time development sync, and `{{packageManagerRunCommand}} test` for local schema and function checks. Deploy Backend functions with Convex's documented deployment workflow.
{{/if}}

## Build Preparation

Use the local Tenkit CLI when you are preparing native projects for a real App Variant build or local native run:

```bash
{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} build
{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} reset
{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} doctor
```

`{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} build` derives the single App Variant, asks for App Variant Environment and platform, pulls EAS environment variables into `.env.local`, verifies that the pulled `APP_VARIANT_SLUG` matches the App Variant, and then runs `expo prebuild --clean` for that native identity. Runtime Tenant selection remains runtime behavior and is not part of Build Preparation.

Before using build preparation:

1. Set `EXPO_OWNER` in `src/constants/project-config.ts` to your Expo account or organization. It starts blank on purpose, so build preparation fails clearly instead of using Tenkit's Expo account.
2. Create or link an EAS Project for the App Variant.
3. Paste the EAS Project ID into `src/constants/app-variant.ts`.
4. Add `APP_VARIANT_SLUG={{appVariants.[0].slug}}` to the App Variant's EAS environment.

A new App Variant Slug can seed creation of a new EAS Project. This project generates `{{appVariants.[0].slug}}` as its App Variant Slug.

When the App Variant is linked to an existing EAS Project, that linked EAS Project is authoritative for owner and Slug. Run `eas init` to reconcile the generated local values with EAS.

For non-interactive CI usage, pass the selected values explicitly:

```bash
{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} build --env production --platform ios
```

`{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} reset` prepares the App Variant for the development environment on both native platforms.

## Run the Prepared App

After build preparation has created native projects for the selected App Variant, start Metro:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} start
```

Then run the prepared native project:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} ios
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} android
```

## App Variant Config

The generated app has one App Variant defined in:

```text
src/constants/app-variant.ts
```

Inspect the resolved public Expo config for the default App Variant:

```bash
{{packageManagerRunCommand}} expo:config
```

Inspect the App Variant explicitly:

```bash
APP_VARIANT_SLUG={{appVariants.[0].slug}} {{packageManagerRunCommand}} expo:config
```

For repeated local work, copy `.env.example` to `.env.local` and set `APP_VARIANT_SLUG={{appVariants.[0].slug}}` there. `.env.local` is ignored by git and may also be rewritten by `{{packageManagerTenkitCommand}} build` after it pulls values from EAS.

## Runtime Tenants

Runtime Tenant records are bundled as source data:

```text
src/constants/runtime-tenants.ts
```

Runtime Tenant Access lives on the App Variant in:

```text
src/constants/app-variant.ts
```

Runtime code can use `src/lib/runtime-tenant-access.ts` to validate Runtime Tenant Access, resolve the default Runtime Tenant, and resolve selectable Runtime Tenants in allowed Runtime Tenant ID order.

The generated app includes a Settings tab with a Runtime Tenant picker when Runtime Tenant Access is selectable.
The selected Runtime Tenant is persisted in local app preferences with MMKV so it survives navigation and app restarts.

## App Variant Assets

Each App Variant has its own asset folder:

```text
assets/<app-variant-slug>/
```

Required App Variant assets:

- `icons/icon.png`
- `icons/android-icon-foreground.png`
- `icons/android-icon-background.png`
- `icons/android-icon-monochrome.png`
- `icons/splash-icon-light.png`
- `icons/splash-icon-dark.png`
- `icons/favicon.png`
- `app.icon/icon.json`

Shared product assets that are not part of native app identity belong in `assets/_global/`.

